// ═══ CompletableFuture uses commonPool by default ═══
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> compute());       // runs on commonPool!
CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> process());           // runs on commonPool!
cf.thenApplyAsync(fn);                                 // runs on commonPool!

// Override with custom pool:
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> compute(), myPool); // runs on myPool!
cf.thenApplyAsync(fn, myPool);                          // runs on myPool!

// ═══ parallelStream uses commonPool ═══
list.parallelStream().map(this::process).toList();  // runs on commonPool!

// Override: submit to custom ForkJoinPool
ForkJoinPool custom = new ForkJoinPool(8);
List<Result> results = custom.submit(() ->
    list.parallelStream().map(this::process).toList()
).join();  // runs on CUSTOM pool!

// ═══ HOW CF uses ForkJoinPool (NOT fork/join style!) ═══
// CompletableFuture does NOT split tasks recursively.
// It just submits Runnable/Callable to the pool as a regular executor.
// The work-stealing helps: if one task finishes fast, that thread
// picks up the next queued task from another thread's deque.

// ═══ HOW parallelStream uses ForkJoinPool (true fork/join!) ═══
// Spliterator.trySplit() → splits data into halves
// Each half becomes a ForkJoinTask → submitted to pool
// Workers process chunks → work-stealing balances load
// Results merged (Collector/reduce)

// ═══ commonPool configuration ═══
// Default parallelism: cores - 1
// Override: -Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=16
// Thread factory: -Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.threadFactory=...
// Exception handler: -Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.exceptionHandler=...
